> Check the domain transform without fitting a model

The native transform is out(y,x)=1-in(H-1-x,y), a90-degree rotation followed by
intensity inversion. Its inverse orientation is1-in(x,W-1-y). It accepts positive
grayscale NHWC images, including rectangular shapes and signed counterfactuals,
without clipping. The supplied3x3 image below has values0/8 through8/8, so its
binary fractions rotate/invert back bit exactly. General Float32 pixels need
not: cancellation in1-(1-x) can change their bits. The data split therefore
retains exact original_B images for diagnostic targets, rather than recreating
them by numerically inverting transformed B.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cycle_rotation.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n      = dm::nn;
  auto original    = n::reshape(n::arange(0, 9, 1, n::DType::Float64) / 8., {1, 3, 3, 1});
  auto transformed = n::models::RotateInvertGrayscale(original),
       inverse     = n::models::RotateInvertGrayscale(transformed, true);
  if (!n::equal_bits(original, inverse))
    throw std::runtime_error("binary-fraction inverse orientation");
  n::save_image_grid(
      n::concat({original, transformed, inverse}, 0),
      "build/cycle_rotation.png",
      3,
      0,
      1,
      4,
      220 / 255.,
      40);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/cycle_rotation.png)
// result_end
\caption(Left: supplied0/8-to8/8 row-major image. Middle: native rotation/inversion. Right: inverse orientation. These are supplied pixels, not generated samples.)
\alttext(Three supplied3x3 grayscale panels show the original ramp, rotated/inverted ramp and exactly recovered binary-fraction original.)

> Split identities by observed class order, then use independent batch orders

For a balanced N-row pool, A takes the first N/20 observed rows of every class
and B takes the remaining N/20, after rotation/inversion. Both gathers preserve
the original global order. Unique original source IDs establish disjointness;
the split does not manufacture aligned training pairs. The native checked MNIST
loader separately verifies original source provenance and exact pixel uniqueness
across pools. The split helper itself checks only its supplied pool's metadata.

The next toy uses20 supplied IDs and placeholder zero images: two observations
per class. Even IDs enter A and odd IDs B. It explains the metadata rule and does
not claim uniqueness of those placeholder pixels. The real image example comes
later. Labels build balanced domains and diagnostics; they are not network inputs.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cycle_domain_toy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n  = dm::nn;
  auto ids     = n::arange(0, 20, 1, n::DType::Int64),
       labels  = n::cast(n::arange(0, 20, 1, n::DType::Float64) / 2., n::DType::Int32);
  auto domains = n::models::SplitCycleDomains(n::zeros({20, 28, 28, 1}), labels, ids);
  std::vector<double> classes(10);
  std::iota(classes.begin(), classes.end(), 0.);
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.y_limits(-1, 13)
      .points(domains.ids_a.to_vector<double>(), classes, "A first occurrence", {37, 99, 235}, 4)
      .points(domains.ids_b.to_vector<double>(), classes, "B second occurrence", {220, 38, 38}, 4)
      .x_label("Supplied original identity; placeholder image pixels are all zero")
      .y_label("Metadata class, used only to partition rows")
      .title("Separate identities do not create paired targets")
      .save_svg("build/cycle_domain_toy.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/cycle_domain_toy.svg)
// result_end
\caption(All20 supplied identities appear once. Each class contributes one different ID to each domain; no model is fitted in this metadata illustration.)
\alttext(Ten even-ID A points and ten odd-ID B points show the first and second observed identities for every class.)

> Capture both partial vectors before the first generator write

A smooth scalar analogue makes the scheduling issue hand-checkable. Let a cycle
have scalar gains u,v and loss.5*(u*v-1)^2. At u=1,v=2, the joint partials are
(2,1). A supplied SGD step of size.1 changes u to.8. Recomputing the v partial
then gives(.8*2-1)*.8=.48, rather than the captured1. Applying the two captured
partials gives v=1.9; the sequential recomputation would give1.952. These are
different methods, without a general claim that one step decreases a nonlinear
objective more. The toy uses a squared smooth cycle term; the fitted model uses
mean L1 cycles, but its generator coupling has the same capture requirement.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cycle_joint_toy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  n::ParameterStore pu(1, n::DType::Float64), pv(2, n::DType::Float64);
  auto              u = pu.get("u", {1}, n::init::ones());
  pv.get("v", {1}, n::init::ones());
  pv.set("v", n::full({1}, 2., n::DType::Float64));
  auto               v        = pv.at("v");
  auto               loss     = .5 * n::square(u * v - 1.);
  auto               captured = n::grad(loss, {u, v});
  n::OptimizerConfig config;
  config.kind = n::OptimizerKind::Sgd;
  n::Optimizer ou(pu, .1, config), ov(pv, .1, config);
  ou.step({captured[0]});
  auto wrong = n::grad(.5 * n::square(pu.at("u") * v - 1.), {v})[0];
  ov.step({captured[1]});
  if (std::abs(captured[0].item() - 2) > 1e-14 || std::abs(captured[1].item() - 1) > 1e-14 ||
      std::abs(wrong.item() - .48) > 1e-14 || std::abs(pv.at("v").item() - 1.9) > 1e-14)
    throw std::runtime_error("joint scalar snapshot");
  auto sweep      = n::arange(0, 36, 1, n::DType::Float64) / 50. + .5;
  auto derivative = (2. * sweep - 1.) * sweep;
  auto p          = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.y_limits(-.1, 2.7)
      .lines(
          sweep.to_vector<double>(),
          derivative.to_vector<double>(),
          "v partial at fixed2",
          {37, 99, 235},
          2)
      .points({1.}, {1.}, "Original u", {16, 145, 74}, 5)
      .points({.8}, {.48}, "After u write", {220, 38, 38}, 5)
      .x_label("Supplied first gain u, second gain v held at2")
      .y_label("Partial of.5*(u*v-1)^2 with respect to v")
      .title("The second gradient changes after the first parameter write")
      .save_svg("build/cycle_joint_toy.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/cycle_joint_toy.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The plotted curve holds v=2. The native two-store toy captures both derivatives before its first write, then applies the saved v vector despite the different recomputed control.)
\alttext(A supplied scalar derivative curve has value1 at u1 and.48 after u moves to.8, illustrating the changed snapshot.)

> Preserve full G adversarial means and half-scaled critic means

For raw patch maps, each critic uses.5*(mean((real-1)^2)+mean(fake^2)). Each G
adversarial term is mean((fake_score-1)^2), without.5. The shared objective is
(adversarial_a+adversarial_b)+(10*(cycle_loss_a+cycle_loss_b)+
5*(identity_loss_a+identity_loss_b)), with all L1 terms averaging pixels separately
inside their own domain. This parenthesization preserves source Float32 arithmetic.
At zero critic scores each D loss is.5 and the two G adversarial terms sum to2.

The next untrained toy supplies constant A=0,B=1 and sets every critic leaf to
zero. Its six plotted terms check those scales and keep the two weighted image
penalties visible. It is separate from the seeded fitted run below.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cycle_loss_toy.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  namespace m = n::models;
  n::ParameterStore ab(6203), ba(6211), da(6217), db(6221);
  auto              a = n::zeros({1, 28, 28, 1}), b = n::ones(a.shape());
  m::CycleGan(ab, ba, da, db, a, b);
  for (auto* p : {&da, &db})
    for (auto name : p->names())
      p->set(name, n::zeros(p->at(name).shape()));
  auto out = m::CycleGan(ab, ba, da, db, a, b);
  if (out.d_a_loss.item() != .5 || out.d_b_loss.item() != .5 || out.adversarial_a.item() != 1 ||
      out.adversarial_b.item() != 1)
    throw std::runtime_error("full G/half D scales");
  std::vector<double> values{
      out.d_a_loss.item(),
      out.d_b_loss.item(),
      (out.adversarial_a + out.adversarial_b).item(),
      (10. * (out.cycle_loss_a + out.cycle_loss_b)).item(),
      (5. * (out.identity_loss_a + out.identity_loss_b)).item(),
      out.g_loss.item()};
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.y_limits(0, 26)
      .points({1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}, values, "Untrained toy", {37, 99, 235}, 5)
      .x_label("1 D_a;2 D_b;3 G adversarial sum;4 cycle x10;5 identity x5;6 G total")
      .y_label("Supplied-constant objective component")
      .title("Two critics and one shared generator objective use distinct scales")
      .save_svg("build/cycle_loss_toy.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
\image(figures/cycle_loss_toy.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Zeroing critic parameters is an explicit toy intervention. The full experiment preserves the source normal initialization and trains both critics.)
\alttext(Six untrained objective components show half-scaled critics, a full adversarial sum and explicit cycle/identity weighted contributions.)

> A zero cycle alone does not identify the intended correspondence

For supplied scalar sets A={0,1},B={2,3}, both G(a)=a+2 with inverse F(b)=b-2,
and G(a)=3-a with inverse F(b)=3-b, match the same two-point target set and have
zero cycles. They disagree about which target belongs to a source. This toy
isolates cycle/distribution ambiguity and omits the fitted identity term. Adding
an identity penalty is useful regularization but does not turn an unconverged
cycle loss into proof of a particular known mapping.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cycle_ambiguity.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  auto a = n::tensor({0., 1.}, {2}, n::DType::Float64), forward = a + 2., reverse = 3. - a;
  if (n::max(n::abs((forward - 2.) - a)).item() != 0 ||
      n::max(n::abs((3. - reverse) - a)).item() != 0)
    throw std::runtime_error("two exact supplied cycles");
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.y_limits(1.5, 4.5)
      .lines(a.to_vector<double>(), forward.to_vector<double>(), "Add2", {37, 99, 235}, 2)
      .points(a.to_vector<double>(), forward.to_vector<double>(), "", {37, 99, 235}, 5)
      .lines(a.to_vector<double>(), reverse.to_vector<double>(), "Reverse to3-a", {220, 38, 38}, 2)
      .points(a.to_vector<double>(), reverse.to_vector<double>(), "", {220, 38, 38}, 5)
      .x_label("Supplied abstract A scalar, not an image pixel")
      .y_label("Supplied abstract B scalar")
      .title("Two inverse pairs have the same marginal sets and zero cycles")
      .save_svg("build/cycle_ambiguity.svg");
}
```
